"""
## Purpose and audience
Runner-discovery probe B: a minimal always-green spec that sorts late in
lexicographic order, pairing with a_spec.spl to bracket a directory run.
For test-runner operators probing discovery, ordering, and verdict
plumbing without depending on any product module beyond std.math.
## Operator workflow
./src/compiler_rust/target/bootstrap/simple run test/zz_probe/b_spec.spl
Expect outcome=OK with exactly 1 passed example and exit code 0.
## Compatibility and limitations
Deliberately tiny: it exercises the spec harness and one std.math call, so
a failure here indicts the runner or the harness, not product logic. The
`it` name is kept as the single letter `y` because ordering probes may
refer to it.
## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
Rerun the single file above and compare the example count and verdict
against the oracle line. A red here usually means a runner or harness
regression; check the runner changelog before touching this file.
"""
# Lifecycle: doc/01_research/infra/sspec/modern_sspec_typed_evidence_research_2026-08-08.md ;
# doc/03_plan/infra/testing/sspec_prevention_mock_plan_2026-08-07.md ;
# doc/04_architecture/test/00_test_architecture.md ;
# doc/05_design/infra/test_runner/unstable_mode_scope_finding_2026-08-17.md
# @manual_section: runner discovery probes

use std.spec.step
use std.math.{sign_i64}

describe "b":
    it "y":
        # @req REQ-TEST-RUNNER-PROBE-002
        # @capture(statistics): sign_i64 sign readback
        step("assert the sign of a negative integer")
        expect(sign_i64(-5)).to_equal(-1)  # oracle: sign of a negative integer is -1
